Implement it. Order of error checks: check for letters first, then punctuation, then digit count. Note: "523-abc-7890" → "letters not permitted". "523-@:!-7890" → "punctuations not permitted". Digits are 10. So check the characters first.

Implementation:

```python
class PhoneNumber:
    def __init__(self, number):
        for ch in number:
            if ch.isalpha():
                raise ValueError("letters not permitted")
        digits = ""
        for ch in number:
            if ch.isdigit():
                digits += ch
            elif ch in " -().":
                continue
            else:
                raise ValueError("punctuations not permitted")
        if len(digits) == 11:
            if not digits.startswith("1"):
                raise ValueError("11 digits must start with 1")
            digits = digits[1:]
        if len(digits) < 10:
            raise ValueError("must not be fewer than 10 digits")
        if len(digits) > 10:
            raise ValueError("must not be greater than 11 digits")
        if digits[0] == "0":
            raise ValueError("area code cannot start with zero")
        if digits[0] == "1":
            raise ValueError("area code cannot start with one")
        if digits[3] == "0":
            raise ValueError("exchange code cannot start with zero")
        if digits[3] == "1":
            raise ValueError("exchange code cannot start with one")
        self.number = digits

    @property
    def area_code(self):
        return self.number[:3]

    def pretty(self):
        return f"({self.area_code})-{self.number[3:6]}-{self.number[6:]}"
```

Wait — the test uses `number.number` as an attribute access, not a method. `PhoneNumber("...").number` — so `number` is an attribute. Okay, I set `self.number = digits`.

Edge case: 12-digit input like "321234567890" (12 digits) → "must not be greater than 11 digits". My code: len==12, doesn't start with 1? "321234567890" starts with 3, len is 12 — my code only strips when len==11. Then len>10 → "must not be greater than 11 digits". Good.

What about 11 digits starting with 1 but with area code 0? "1 (023) 456-7890" → digits "10234567890", strip → "0234567890", area code starts with 0 → error. Good.

What about 11 digits starting with 1 but with fewer than 10 digits after stripping? Impossible — stripping 1 from 11 digits gives 10 digits.

Also, what about fewer than 10 digits AND starting with 1? For example "123456789" (9 digits) → "must not be fewer than 10 digits". Good.

Edge case: 11 digits starting with 1, but… no problem.

One nuance: what about 11 digits starting with 1 where the area code starts with 1? For example "11234567890" → strip → "1234567890" → area code starts with 1 → error. Good.

